## Email change
A signed-in user changes their address on `/profile/account` (`change-email-form.tsx`): the new address and the current password go to `authClient.changeEmail`. Nothing changes when the form is sent: Better Auth signs a link to the new address and hands it to `emailVerification.sendVerificationEmail` (in `auth.ts`), which calls `deliverLink({ kind: 'change-email', ... })` in `{{file}}`, the one place every link leaves the server ({{links}}). Without a mailer the link is only in the server console (`[change-email] <new email> → <link>`), and in production the same `No mailer is configured` warning is printed. Opening the link (`/api/auth/verify-email`) switches the address, marks it verified, and redirects to `/profile/account?emailChange=confirmed`; the profile and the user menu read the user from the session on each request, so both show the new address at once. A link that fails redirects to the same page with `?error=<code>`, which `emailChangeNotice` (`apps/web/src/lib/auth/email-change.ts`) turns into a message, and `EmailChangeNoticeAlert` drops the query once shown.

The guard is the `emailChangeGuard` plugin (`packages/auth/src/email-change-guard.ts`). What it protects, and what it does not:
- **A stolen session cannot take the account.** `/change-email` needs the current password, checked on the server against the stored hash before anything else, so the answer for a wrong password is the same whether or not the new address is taken. Without it a thief with a session could ask for their own address, open their own link and use the password reset there. A thief who also knows the password can still do this, as they could change the password. Better Auth's rate limit is the only limit on guessing the password through this route, and it is kept in memory, {{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}per Worker isolate{{else}}per server process{{/if}}, as for `/sign-in`: a brake, not a guarantee.
- **The link only works in a browser signed in to the account.** Left alone, Better Auth creates a session for whoever opens the link when the browser has none, so an address typed with a typo would hand the account to its owner. A link opened without a session goes back to the callback with `error=SIGN_IN_REQUIRED` and nothing changes, and signing in returns to that page with its query. The callback is checked with `isTrustedOrigin` as Better Auth checks it, so a hostile one answers 403, never a redirect. The plugin watches `/verify-email`, which this blueprint only uses for email change: re-check it when adding another email verification flow.
- **The old address is told.** Once the change is committed, `deliverLink({ kind: 'email-changed', ... })` writes `[email-changed] <old email> → <site>` to the old address. It is a notice, not an undo.
- **An address taken before the link is opened** ends on the callback with `error=EMAIL_TAKEN`, not on a database failure.
- The form shows the same message whether or not the new address already has an account, because Better Auth answers success without sending anything for a taken one. The delivery runs through `advanced.backgroundTasks` ({{#if (has "stack" "tanstack-start")}}`apps/web/src/lib/auth/auth.ts` hands it the `waitUntil` of the request, which the Nitro node server gives it and waits for on shutdown{{else if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}`getAuth()` in `apps/web/src/lib/server.ts` hands it the Worker's `waitUntil`{{else}}`apps/web/src/lib/auth/auth.ts` hands it `after` of `next/server`{{/if}}), so a free address does not take longer to answer than a taken one once a real mailer is plugged in. Keep that when replacing the instance, and do not await a mailer anywhere else in the request.
- `updateEmailWithoutVerification` is left off: with it a stolen session could replace the address with no proof. `sendChangeEmailConfirmation` is not used either, so there is one link, to the new address. Do not enable `emailVerification.sendOnSignUp` or `sendOnSignIn` without reading the link flow again: the same function would then also send a sign-up verification.
- `disabledPaths` removes `/send-verification-email`: enabling `sendVerificationEmail` makes Better Auth expose that public route, which would send a verification link to any unverified account on request.
{{#if (has "stack" "tanstack-start")}}
- **A Lambda gives the request no `waitUntil`** (the `sst` and `terraform-aws` deployments run Nitro with the `aws-lambda` preset) and freezes the process when the handler returns, so a delivery nobody waits for could be lost. There `handleAuthRequest` (`apps/web/src/lib/auth/auth.ts`, the handler of `routes/api/auth/$.ts`) holds the answer until the deliveries of the request are done. The cost is a timing difference: an address that has an account answers later than one that has not (nothing is sent for it), by the time the mailer takes, so once a real mailer is plugged in a Lambda deployment can tell the two apart by the response time. A mailer that answers in milliseconds narrows the gap, and handing the link to a queue (SQS, ...) instead of sending it closes it. Do not call `auth.handler` from another route without `handleAuthRequest`.
{{/if}}
{{#if admin}}
- `user.role = admin` can still change any email through the admin API: it stays equivalent to root.
{{/if}}
